<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Historical Building.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Assist in explaining the rules of a classroom game. Concept: Guide peers through setting up a classroom activity.
            <br>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist in explaining the rules of an art-related game with basic actions (e.g., gesturing "Draw something blue").
            Can guide peers through setting up a classroom activity by demonstrating steps (e.g., showing how to fold paper for "First, fold the paper").
            Can provide support by explaining each part of a craft activity (e.g., points to the markers and says, "Now add colors here").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing images of famous historical buildings and local Panamanian crafts (e.g., molas, tagua carvings). Students briefly recall vocabulary. The teacher then introduces a quick "Art Term Charades" game. Students will take turns miming an art-related word (e.g., "paint," "sculpt," "mola") and their peers guess. This warms up their ability to explain/interpret with actions, leading into mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain the rules of "Art Term Charades." "First, one student chooses a word like 'sculpt.' Then, you act it out without speaking. Your classmates guess. The person who guesses correctly goes next!" The teacher also models how to guide peers, using gestures and simple phrases: "First, fold the paper like this. Look, I'm folding it." (demonstrating).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we speak during charades?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we do after someone guesses the word?" (Expected answer: "The person who guessed acts next.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated guide (can be a printed handout or digital slide) titled "How to Create a Mini Historical Building Model." The guide uses simple imperatives and present/past simple to describe the materials and steps, and a brief description of a famous Panamanian artist who "has created many detailed sculptures."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example excerpts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, gather your materials: paper, scissors, glue, and markers. We visited an artist last week who uses similar tools."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next, cut out the basic shape of your building. This artist has designed many unique structures."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Then, fold along the lines and glue the tabs. The building is a two-story structure, with a rectangular design."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Finally, add colors and details with markers. Our local artist, Maria Rodriguez, has created beautiful sculptures inspired by old buildings."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing creations: "The building is a two-story structure, with a rectangular design and white stone facades." Describing achievements of artists: "This artist has created beautiful sculptures." Describing how to create something: "First, you prepare the materials and soak the fibers."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are we learning to make today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The artist uses paint to create the sculptures." "You add colors at the beginning of the process."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using imperatives for instructions, present perfect for achievements, and present/past simple for descriptions. Target vocabulary: <i>artist, craft, building, sculpture, create, design, colorful, detailed.</i> Pronunciation focus: Syllable stress in <i>sculpture</i> (/skʌlp.tʃər/) and <i>design</i> (/dɪ'zaɪn/). Identification and articulation of inflectional endings (-ed, -ing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Fill-in-the-Instructions" (5 min)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a handout with incomplete instructions for a simple craft (e.g., drawing a mola pattern or building a simple paper building). They complete the sentences using given verbs in the imperative form (e.g., "First, ___ (draw) a square shape." "Then, ___ (cut) the fabric."). Also, they complete sentences using present perfect about an artist: "This artist ___ (create) many unique molas." Teacher guides pronunciation of stressed syllables and -ed/-ing endings.</p></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Sequence the Steps" (5 min)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In pairs, students get jumbled steps for a simple "Historical Building Mural" activity (e.g., "Add vibrant colors," "Sketch the building outlines," "Prepare the canvas"). They must reorder the steps logically and write them down using sequence words (First, Next, Then, Finally) and imperatives. They then practice explaining the sequence to their partner, focusing on clear articulation.</p></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Mediation: "Explain the Craft" (5 min)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each pair receives a simple drawing of a craft (e.g., how to make a paper basket or a simple tagua carving design with sequential steps). One student explains the steps to their partner using imperatives and gestures, acting as the 'guide'. The partner listens and asks clarifying questions (e.g., "Do I fold it like this?"). They then switch roles. Teacher circulates, monitoring for correct use of grammar and pronunciation of words like "sculpture" and "design," and providing support.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their "Digital Art Exhibition" project groups. Their task is to prepare a short oral "explanation and guidance" session for a specific part of their digital exhibition to another group of peers. The focus is on mediation: explaining concepts and guiding steps related to their chosen historical building/artist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group "Exhibition Stand" Presentation (5-7 minutes per group).</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group selects one "exhibit" from their Digital Art Exhibition project (e.g., a specific artist's work, a particular historical building, or a local craft).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must explain their chosen exhibit to another group. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Explaining a historical building:</b> "This is the San Jose Church. It's a very old building. Its design is colonial, and it has unique golden altars. Local artisans have created detailed carvings for its interior."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Explaining an artist's achievement:</b> "This artist, Elena Garcia, has created many vibrant murals about Panamanian history. She has also designed unique tagua carvings. Yesterday, we learned about her studio."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guiding through a craft related to the exhibit (simulated):</b> "Imagine we are making a mini mola inspired by this artist. First, prepare your fabric. Next, draw geometric shapes. Now, cut the fabric pieces carefully." (Students can use visual aids/posters from their project).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should actively use gestures and clear language to assist understanding, demonstrating the mediation skills. They should use present perfect ("has created," "has designed"), present/past simple, and imperatives.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining, guiding peers, active listening, using target vocabulary (<i>artist, craft, building, mural, sculpture, exhibition, creative, unique</i>), and grammatical structures (present perfect, imperatives) in a collaborative setting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual and group performance during the "Exhibition Stand" presentations. The teacher uses a simple rubric to track student's ability to mediate information effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, within their group presentation, must demonstrate their ability to either:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain a concept related to their chosen exhibit (e.g., describing "This Is a Historical Building" using specific features).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guide a peer through a simple simulated step of an art activity or the process of curating a digital exhibit section (e.g., "First, click here. Then, upload your image.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provide support by explaining each part of their exhibit to ensure understanding.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, correct syllable stress (e.g., <i>sculpture, design</i>) and inflectional endings (-ed, -ing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Appropriate use of words like <i>artist, craft, building, mural, sculpture, exhibition, creative, detailed, vibrant</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanation/guidance:</b> Explanations are easy to follow; steps are clearly demonstrated or described using imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and engagement:</b> Actively assists peers in understanding and setting up, responds to questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct use of present perfect, present simple, past simple, and imperatives.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of our digital exhibition was I able to explain clearly today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase I used to help my classmates understand something?"</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help my classmates understand our project today? Was it by explaining words, showing steps, or something else?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining or guiding, and what could I do better next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively use imperatives and present perfect to explain and guide? Were the explanations clear and well-structured?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific mediation strategies (e.g., gestures, rephrasing, pointing) did students use, and how well did they work?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments are needed for supporting students in mediation tasks, perhaps providing more sentence starters or visual cues for future lessons?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "This Is a Historical Building" theme. The next lesson (Lesson 1 of a new theme) will introduce new vocabulary and listening skills related to a different cultural or social topic, building on the collaborative and communicative skills developed in this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>